[# Section 0a — Why CX. Authored 2026-09-28 (RULED: DOCS-49 wave 1, DOCS-47,
   RS-30). The case for CX in eight claims, each stated in our own voice and
   each proved by a before/after panel: a [pair wrong= right=] block names two
   cases of conformance/llm/antipatterns.cxd that the guide build replays
   against the running cx — the form that fails on the left, the CX form on
   the right — so nothing on this page is a hand-typed "before", and a claim
   whose evidence moves stops the build by name.

   The docs speak for CX (the docs-voice rule): first person plural, no
   third-party voice, no comparison to a language this page does not need.
   Cross-links use [[anchor]] form. File extension .cxd — data-only source. #]

[section id=why group='Start here' n='0a' title='Why CX'

  [intro
    """**TL;DR — we built CX so that the data is the program's own shape.**
       One bracketed syntax is the document, the query, the program and the
       compiler's own tree, and everything we promise below follows from
       holding that line: numbers that stay exact, effects you grant rather
       than inherit, errors and absence that are values, patterns that cannot
       blow up, dispatch on the shape of the data. Each claim on this page is
       a pair of conformance cases this guide's `cx` replayed when the page
       was built — the form that fails on the left, ours on the right. If a
       claim ever stops holding, this page stops building."""]

  [child id=why-exact n=0a.1 title='We keep exact numbers exact'
    [body
      """A fraction literal is a decimal — exact, base-10, arbitrary precision —
         and arithmetic keeps it one. Money stays money. Mixing a decimal with
         a binary float is refused out loud, naming the one bridge between
         them, rather than rounded in silence into a column that was never
         supposed to round."""]
    [pair wrong=ap-decimal-float-mix-wrong right=ap-decimal-float-mix-right]]

  [child id=why-effects n=0a.2 title='We grant effects; nothing is inherited'
    [body
      """A program reads nothing, writes nothing, dials nothing and reads no
         clock until the command line grants it. The refusal is an ordinary
         value that names the flag which would allow the effect — not a
         crash, not a silent empty result — so the same program is safe to
         run first and to trust second. See [[capabilities]] for the whole
         grant surface."""]
    [pair wrong=ap-effect-ungranted-wrong right=ap-effect-granted-right]]

  [child id=why-errors n=0a.3 title='Our errors are values you match on'
    [body
      """There is no `try`. An `[err]` is a value that flows out of whatever
         is built from it, and the shape you wanted is a pattern you match
         against: the single-arm `[?match]` destructures or refuses with the
         language's own diagnostic, so a bespoke error code and its wording
         stop being yours to maintain. The multi-arm form is for genuinely
         several shapes, not for one shape and a safety net."""]
    [pair wrong=ap-match-else-rethrow-wrong right=ap-match-else-rethrow-right]]

  [child id=why-absence n=0a.4 title='Absence is a value we can ask about'
    [body
      """A missing key, an out-of-range read, an empty match set — each has a
         defined, distinguishable answer, and absence is not emptiness.
         `[$present]` asks whether something is there at all; `[$exists]`
         asks whether it has contents. A guard that confuses the two takes
         the wrong branch at exit 0 with nothing printed, which is the one
         outcome we refuse to ship; the right predicate makes the question
         explicit."""]
    [pair wrong=ap-exists-as-presence-wrong right=ap-exists-as-presence-right]]

  [child id=why-regex n=0a.5 title='Our regular expressions cannot blow up'
    [body
      """`re` is RE2: a pattern that compiles runs in time linear in its
         input, whatever the input. The constructs that would need
         backtracking — a backreference, a lookaround — are refused when the
         pattern compiles, so a hostile string can never turn a match into a
         denial of service. The nested quantifier that kills a backtracking
         engine answers here in linear time."""]
    [pair wrong=ap-regex-backtracking-wrong right=ap-regex-linear-right]]

  [child id=why-shape n=0a.6 title='We dispatch on the shape of the data'
    [body
      """A closed set of values is declared where it travels, in a schema, and
         a `[?match]` over it says so with `[of TypeName]`: the linter then
         names a missing member, an out-of-set value refuses at the boundary,
         and `[else]` goes back to meaning only what it says. Bare strings and
         a catch-all arm report a member you forgot as `unknown`, at exit 0,
         forever."""]
    [pair wrong=ap-open-string-dispatch-wrong right=ap-open-string-dispatch-right]]

  [child id=why-flat n=0a.7 title='We bind flat — readable on day one'
    [body
      """`[?let]` is sequential: each binding sees the ones before it, so
         three dependent bindings are one form, not three nested scopes. The
         two halves below are output-identical; the flat one is the house
         form, and it is the one a reader parses at a glance."""]
    [pair wrong=ap-nested-let-wrong right=ap-nested-let-right]]

  [child id=why-operators n=0a.8 title='Our operators are values — batteries included'
    [body
      """An operator with holes is the operator's value: `[+ _ _]` goes
         wherever a callable goes — a `[using …]` clause, a higher-order
         standard-library parameter, an entry in an operator table a runtime
         token looks up. Nothing stands between the operator and the slot, and
         the arity is visible in the holes. The adapter lambda is pure
         ceremony, and we do not make you write it."""]
    [pair wrong=ap-adapter-lambda-wrong right=ap-adapter-lambda-right]]

  [child id=why-next n=0a.9 title='Where this leads'
    [body
      """Every module we ship is declared once, with its ring and its
         repository, in a registry the build holds the tree to — the figure
         on [[rings]] is drawn from that registry when the page is built, and
         so are the counts on the front page. From here: [[quickstart]] runs a
         first file, a first conversion, a first query and a first schema in
         five minutes; [[rings]] says how much of CX you have to take on;
         [[choosing]] routes you by task."""]]]
